(*
MagTagButtonsNode — the MagTag four-button bring-up node (running-node half of
docs/design/2026-07-23-magtag-buttons-leds-design.md).

Proves the plural pulse seam end to end on real hardware:

  4x GPIO ISR -> pulse_isr demux + debounce -> gpio_target_fire(logical)
              -> pulse_post_isr -> k_msgq -> pumpEvents -> per-button channel
              -> the node's reaction

ONE CHANNEL PER BUTTON (design D1). This needs NO new prim and NO VM change:
src/platform.c's host_gpio_register table is already keyed per LOGICAL PIN, each
slot carrying its own channel id and its own posted message, so four buttons are
just four registrations. That the seam was already per-pin is why the whole
four-button change landed in zephyr/src/main.c and a devicetree overlay.

LOGICAL PIN == THE MAGTAG'S GPIO NUMBER, so these lines read like the silkscreen:
BUTTON_A is GPIO15, B is 14, C is 12, D is 11 (vendor pinout). That mapping is
NOT a coincidence — zephyr/src/main.c's pulse_logical_of() derives the logical pin
from the devicetree spec's own pin number on any board with more than one button,
precisely so a node can name the pin the board is labelled with. Single-button
boards (nRF52840-DK, dongle) still get the historic 13, so PulseNodeDK.nsl is
untouched.

`edge: #falling` is ADVISORY ONLY — src/platform.c's PRIM_pf_gpioPin reads args[1]
and then never uses it (host_gpio_register takes pin/chan/handle). The real edge is
set in the overlay: the buttons are declared GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW | GPIO_PULL_UP (the
MagTag has NO board pull-ups), which makes main.c's GPIO_INT_EDGE_TO_ACTIVE the
falling edge. #falling is written here because it is what physically happens, not
because anything reads it.

DEBOUNCE IS IN C, not here, and deliberately so — see design D3. The short version:
pulse_post_isr already coalesces and sheds, so bounce can neither flood the mailbox
nor kill the node; but coalescing only merges while an entry is still QUEUED, so
after a pump the next bounce edge arrives as another logical press. Debouncing in
nsl would therefore cost a full reaction fire per bounce edge to discard it.

Node shape is PulseNodeDK's (`packageUsing: manifest`, platform arriving as the
`main:` argument); grants no capability, so no `net:` header. Dialect: `[ ... ]
whileTrue:`, never `repeat`.

This node never returns, so — unlike TimerNode — it keeps draining forever and
cannot leak a live repeater.

expect (one line per press, in whatever order the buttons are pressed):
BTN A
BTN B
BTN C
BTN D
*)
class MagTagButtonsNode packageUsing: manifest = () (
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | space gpio btnA btnB btnC btnD |
    space:: platform space.

    btnA:: space channel: 'btn/a'.
    btnB:: space channel: 'btn/b'.
    btnC:: space channel: 'btn/c'.
    btnD:: space channel: 'btn/d'.

    btnA whenever: '' do: [ :m | ('BTN ' , m printString) println ].
    btnB whenever: '' do: [ :m | ('BTN ' , m printString) println ].
    btnC whenever: '' do: [ :m | ('BTN ' , m printString) println ].
    btnD whenever: '' do: [ :m | ('BTN ' , m printString) println ].

    gpio:: platform gpio.
    (* pin: is the MagTag GPIO number == the silkscreen label *)
    gpio pin: 15 edge: #falling post: 'A' on: btnA.
    gpio pin: 14 edge: #falling post: 'B' on: btnB.
    gpio pin: 12 edge: #falling post: 'C' on: btnC.
    gpio pin: 11 edge: #falling post: 'D' on: btnD.

    (* No `gpio fire:` — every edge is a physical press. pumpEvents BLOCKS on the
       pulse mailbox between presses, so the VM idles the CPU rather than spinning. *)
    [ true ] whileTrue: [ platform pumpEvents. space drain ]
  )
) : (
)
